WEBVTT

00:00:13.440 --> 00:00:18.240
Whether a technician is a Porsche certified high voltage technician, HVT,

00:00:18.240 --> 00:00:23.920
or a Porsche certified high voltage expert, HVE, they need to understand the importance

00:00:23.920 --> 00:00:28.780
of personal safety when working with these high voltage, high energy systems.

00:00:28.780 --> 00:00:33.360
For any technician to stay safe, they must understand what personal protective equipment

00:00:33.360 --> 00:00:38.480
or PPE is required and how this works to keep them safe.

00:00:38.480 --> 00:00:43.960
Special gloves, face shields, shoes, shirts, and pants are all required.

00:00:43.960 --> 00:00:48.400
Specific regulations apply to all these things, so it is important for the technician

00:00:48.400 --> 00:00:53.400
to know where to turn to find information on high voltage PPE.

00:00:53.400 --> 00:00:58.000
The Code of Federal Regulations categorizes the general and permanent rules published

00:00:58.000 --> 00:00:59.500
by the federal government.

00:00:59.500 --> 00:01:06.440
It is divided into 50 titles that represent broad areas subject to federal regulation.

00:01:06.440 --> 00:01:10.900
Title 29 addresses labor standards and is where the Occupational Safety

00:01:10.900 --> 00:01:14.280
and Health Administration, or OSHA, general guidelines relative

00:01:14.280 --> 00:01:17.760
to workplace safety are found.

00:01:17.760 --> 00:01:21.080
Within Title 29 are multiple subtitles or parts.

00:01:27.880 --> 00:01:33.240
Part 1910.335 lists safeguards for personnel working in areas

00:01:33.240 --> 00:01:35.880
where potential electrical hazards could exist.

00:01:39.480 --> 00:01:43.200
Safeguards for personnel protection lists the overall requirements

00:01:43.200 --> 00:01:46.880
without detailed specifications for each requirement.

00:01:50.520 --> 00:01:54.440
These are the general guidelines that are based on the more specific recommendations

00:01:54.440 --> 00:01:59.400
from the NFPA 70E standard for electrical safety in the workplace.

00:02:02.680 --> 00:02:06.960
The NFPA is the National Fire Protection Association and is recognized

00:02:06.960 --> 00:02:10.920
as the defining authority on electrical safety in the workplace.

00:02:13.000 --> 00:02:18.200
More information can be found on their website at nfpa.org.

00:02:18.200 --> 00:02:22.200
It is important for the Porsche high voltage technician to be aware of the risks

00:02:22.200 --> 00:02:25.800
when working with potentially lethal electrical current and to be aware

00:02:25.800 --> 00:02:29.840
of measures available to mitigate such risks.

00:02:29.840 --> 00:02:34.400
The physiological effects of electric shock range from a slight tingling sensation

00:02:34.400 --> 00:02:38.480
at current under 1 milliamp to loss of voluntary muscle control

00:02:38.480 --> 00:02:40.720
at current around 16 milliamps.

00:02:40.720 --> 00:02:47.080
The maximum permissible touch voltage is 60 volts for DC and 30 volts for AC.

00:02:47.080 --> 00:02:50.720
Current in excess of 100 milliamps is considered deadly.

00:02:50.720 --> 00:02:55.560
The human body can have resistance as low as 1000 ohms from hand to hand.

00:02:55.560 --> 00:03:00.120
When Ohm's law is used to calculate current with a resistance of 1000 ohms

00:03:00.120 --> 00:03:04.720
and with 300 volts, the result is a lethal 300 milliamps.

00:03:08.600 --> 00:03:12.680
Porsche specific recommendations for workplace electrical safety can be found

00:03:12.680 --> 00:03:14.920
in workshop manual section 2X.

00:03:16.920 --> 00:03:23.440
Document 2X00IN, specifications for preparing a high voltage workplace,

00:03:23.440 --> 00:03:26.800
lists the key points for high voltage safety awareness.

00:03:26.800 --> 00:03:29.560
These are general guidelines for working in areas

00:03:29.560 --> 00:03:33.440
where there could be potential for high voltage exposure.

00:03:33.440 --> 00:03:36.960
Suitable personal protective gear and equipment must be used

00:03:36.960 --> 00:03:39.720
for the duration of the work.

00:03:39.720 --> 00:03:42.520
It must be adapted according to the type of work.

00:03:42.520 --> 00:03:47.680
The voltage level up to 1000 volts AC or 1500 volts DC.

00:03:47.680 --> 00:03:51.560
The risks of electric current flowing through the human body or possible arcs

00:03:51.560 --> 00:03:56.240
in the event of a short circuit and the ambient conditions.

00:03:56.240 --> 00:04:00.400
The personal protective gear must always be checked visually before use

00:04:00.400 --> 00:04:02.920
and must be replaced on a regular basis.

00:04:02.920 --> 00:04:04.640
Observe the national requirements

00:04:04.640 --> 00:04:07.960
and legislation governing personal protective gear and equipment

00:04:07.960 --> 00:04:09.520
and find out all about it.

00:04:09.520 --> 00:04:12.800
Emergency and safety systems are known and available.

00:04:12.800 --> 00:04:15.920
Observe the national requirements and legislation.

00:04:15.920 --> 00:04:17.840
All required tools and measuring

00:04:17.840 --> 00:04:21.480
and test equipment have been approved and tested for this work.

00:04:21.480 --> 00:04:22.880
Testing tools and measuring

00:04:22.880 --> 00:04:26.760
and test equipment must be calibrated every 12 months at the latest

00:04:26.760 --> 00:04:30.080
for standard workshop use.

00:04:30.080 --> 00:04:33.480
Personal protective equipment includes the following items discussed

00:04:33.480 --> 00:04:35.160
in this video.

00:04:35.160 --> 00:04:40.200
Outer clothing can be separate shirt and pants or a one-piece coverall.

00:04:40.200 --> 00:04:44.120
Head and eye protection, gloves and shoes.

00:04:44.120 --> 00:04:49.680
First and foremost, gloves are used to protect against electric shock

00:04:49.680 --> 00:04:54.000
and are often the only PPE required for certain tasks.

00:04:54.000 --> 00:04:57.720
Rubber electrical insulating gloves are rated for voltage protection

00:04:57.720 --> 00:05:03.200
and are categorized by the American Society for Testing and Materials or ASTM.

00:05:03.200 --> 00:05:05.880
Gloves are used to protect against electric shock

00:05:05.880 --> 00:05:10.320
and are often the only PPE required for certain tasks.

00:05:10.320 --> 00:05:15.040
Gloves are classified by voltage protection level as shown on this chart.

00:05:15.040 --> 00:05:19.680
Gloves should be rated well above any maximum potential voltage exposure.

00:05:19.680 --> 00:05:25.320
Class 0 gloves rated for 1000 volts AC or 1500 volts DC are adequate

00:05:25.320 --> 00:05:29.720
for high voltage systems found on Porsche vehicles.

00:05:29.720 --> 00:05:34.240
It is important that your gloves fit properly to retain the necessary dexterity.

00:05:34.240 --> 00:05:38.200
To determine glove size, measure around the widest part of the hand.

00:05:38.200 --> 00:05:39.840
Do not include the thumb.

00:05:39.840 --> 00:05:42.880
The measurement in inches is your glove size.

00:05:42.880 --> 00:05:47.080
Before each use, check the insulated gloves for cracks, tears

00:05:47.080 --> 00:05:51.240
and any other type of damage by performing the following procedure.

00:05:51.240 --> 00:05:54.040
Open the glove fully to allow air to enter.

00:05:54.040 --> 00:05:57.600
Then close the end and roll it up causing the glove to inflate.

00:05:57.600 --> 00:06:00.560
Fold the rolled up opening to ensure a good seal.

00:06:00.560 --> 00:06:02.520
Listen for any air leaks.

00:06:02.520 --> 00:06:07.080
Every six months, the rubber insulated gloves need to be sent to a testing laboratory

00:06:07.080 --> 00:06:10.560
to be electrically tested and recertified for use.

00:06:10.560 --> 00:06:14.400
Your glove supplier will be able to offer this service.

00:06:14.400 --> 00:06:20.120
After gloves pass the recertification testing, a new date is stamped on the glove.

00:06:20.120 --> 00:06:27.200
Pay attention to this date and do not use gloves with a date older than six months from the present.

00:06:28.240 --> 00:06:33.200
Clean, dry cloth glove liners are recommended to be worn inside of the insulating gloves

00:06:33.200 --> 00:06:39.800
for warmth in cold weather and to absorb perspiration in hot weather.

00:06:39.800 --> 00:06:45.960
Ensure a proper fit before continuing with the rubber gloves.

00:06:45.960 --> 00:06:49.120
Next, put on each rubber insulating glove.

00:06:49.120 --> 00:06:53.600
Making sure that shirt or coverall sleeves are securely tucked inside the glove

00:06:53.600 --> 00:06:57.840
and that there are no areas of exposed skin.

00:06:57.840 --> 00:07:06.880
The outer protection gloves are made of leather and are only intended to protect the rubber gloves.

00:07:06.880 --> 00:07:10.000
The rubber gloves protect the technician from high voltage

00:07:10.000 --> 00:07:16.160
and the outer protection gloves protect the rubber gloves from wear and tear while working.

00:07:16.160 --> 00:07:22.960
Place the outer leather protection gloves over the rubber insulating gloves.

00:07:22.960 --> 00:07:26.640
Outer protector gloves may be omitted for class zero gloves

00:07:26.640 --> 00:07:33.600
under limited use conditions where small equipment and parts require unusually good finger dexterity.

00:07:33.600 --> 00:07:37.440
While the gloves worn alone provide protection against electric shock,

00:07:37.440 --> 00:07:42.640
all the clothing worn together with the gloves provides protection against arc flash.

00:07:42.640 --> 00:07:49.360
As shown in previous trainings, this is an extreme hazard that requires serious protective measures to mitigate.

00:07:49.360 --> 00:07:54.800
While it is possible to measure voltage potential, arc flash potential cannot be specifically measured

00:07:54.800 --> 00:07:58.880
so precautions must be taken.

00:07:58.880 --> 00:08:08.000
Under NFPA Standard 70E, Table 130.7 addresses the applicable arc ratings for protective clothing.

00:08:08.000 --> 00:08:13.840
All other clothing beyond the gloves is specifically intended to protect against the hazards of arc flash

00:08:13.840 --> 00:08:18.160
and must be worn when performing work where arc flash is possible.

00:08:18.160 --> 00:08:24.000
Depending on the possible level of arc flash exposure, which is based on the current potential in the system,

00:08:24.000 --> 00:08:28.320
arc flash PPE is categorized in four levels.

00:08:28.320 --> 00:08:34.240
Clothing is arc rated by the ASTM and is expressed as calories per square centimeter.

00:08:34.240 --> 00:08:39.280
Simply stated, an arc rating is a direct measure of that fabric's ability to insulate the wearer

00:08:39.280 --> 00:08:41.840
from second degree burn through the fabric.

00:08:41.840 --> 00:08:46.880
1.2 calories per square centimeter is the threshold for a second degree burn.

00:08:46.880 --> 00:08:49.760
The arc rating will appear on the clothing label.

00:08:49.760 --> 00:08:56.160
The higher the number, the higher the level of arc flash and flammability protection.

00:08:56.160 --> 00:09:02.000
The energy potential within the system will determine the rating required for anyone working on the system.

00:09:02.000 --> 00:09:09.040
Porsche electric vehicles operate at 800 volts and Porsche hybrid vehicles operate at 400 volts.

00:09:09.040 --> 00:09:14.080
This means that a higher level of protection is required when working on BEV models.

00:09:14.080 --> 00:09:17.440
The Porsche special tools and equipment subspace on PPN

00:09:17.440 --> 00:09:22.800
will have all the information needed to help determine the exact PPE required.

00:09:22.800 --> 00:09:38.640
Here, the differences between the PPE kits for 400 volt work and 800 volt work are shown.

00:09:38.640 --> 00:09:46.720
Please refer to NFPA 70E, Table 130.7 for more information.

00:09:46.720 --> 00:09:53.600
Depending on the specific PPE rating, coveralls may be used in place of a separate shirt and pants if desired.

00:09:53.600 --> 00:09:57.040
Shoes must be certified for electrical hazard protection.

00:09:57.040 --> 00:10:04.160
These shoes have insulating properties and should not be confused with anti-static shoes.

00:10:04.160 --> 00:10:09.120
Appropriate shoes will have the EH designation on the label inside the shoe.

00:10:09.120 --> 00:10:12.320
Hard toe, EH rated shoes are recommended.

00:10:12.320 --> 00:10:21.200
Head and face protection should include a face shield, safety glasses, and arc rated balaclava.

00:10:21.200 --> 00:10:30.400
Here we see the complete personal protective equipment for a 400 volt system ready for use.

00:10:30.400 --> 00:10:41.760
Remember to always be prepared for your personal safety when working with high voltage on Porsche electric drive systems.

00:10:41.760 --> 00:10:45.600
Only the appropriate insulated tools from the high voltage tool set

00:10:45.600 --> 00:10:49.760
are to be used when working with high voltage components.

00:10:49.760 --> 00:10:54.720
These tools are fully insulated and are the only tools approved for this use.

00:10:54.720 --> 00:10:57.840
Always refer to workshop manual instructions.

00:10:57.840 --> 00:11:04.640
Inspect your insulated tools for damage before each use and replace any that show signs of damage.

00:11:04.640 --> 00:11:10.880
A simple drop tool in the wrong place could cause arc flash resulting in severe personal injury.

00:11:10.880 --> 00:11:16.960
The proper tools are available for a reason. Please make use of them.

00:11:16.960 --> 00:11:21.680
Personal protective equipment can be purchased from multiple sources.

00:11:21.680 --> 00:11:27.520
These sources are listed on the Porsche Special Tools subspace in PPN mentioned previously.

00:11:27.520 --> 00:11:32.480
Links are provided to the various websites where PPE can be purchased.

00:11:32.480 --> 00:11:36.320
It is the responsibility of the Porsche Center to provide PPE

00:11:36.320 --> 00:11:42.000
with the exception of shoes which are the responsibility of the individual technician.

00:11:42.000 --> 00:11:46.880
All items shown in this video were sourced from 70E Solutions.

00:11:46.880 --> 00:11:52.160
When working with high voltage systems, the proper PPE is the last defense against accidents

00:11:52.160 --> 00:11:55.680
that can lead to severe injury and even death.

00:11:55.680 --> 00:12:02.000
Remember, the HV rated gloves are the primary defense against accidental electric shock.

00:12:02.000 --> 00:12:08.400
The arc flash clothing combined with the gloves is to prevent severe burns that result from arc flash.

00:12:08.400 --> 00:12:13.440
Make sure to follow all warnings and regulations laid out in the Porsche workshop manual

00:12:13.440 --> 00:12:17.280
as well as those found in the NFPA 70E standard.

00:12:17.280 --> 00:12:21.840
In the end, common sense and an understanding and respect for high voltage systems

00:12:21.840 --> 00:12:26.160
and how they work will help the technician work safely.
